  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 102 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nBeginning on Tuesday, the aforementioned upper level ridge over\nthe western CONUS will begin to flatten out as an upper low over\nCanada begins to push an associated upper shortwave trough\nthrough the northern Plains. Tuesday and much of Wednesday\nshould remain dry however as high pressure still dominates over\nthe area. Temperatures continue to climb, with the majority of\nthe CWA reaching the low to mid 90s by Wednesday (and even the\nupper 90s for areas along/southwest of I-49). Humidity levels\nshould remain marginal, however some locations that reach the\nupper 90s could see Heat Indices surpass 100 degrees. Current\nguidance suggests these areas remain below Heat Advisory\ncriteria though. \n\nAs we get further into the afternoon and into the evening on \nWednesday, a surface low associated with the aforementioned \nupper level shortwave trough will continue to push through the\nGreat Lakes region, with a cold front sweeping southwest through\nnorth/central MO and central KS. As the front continues to push\nsouth towards the area during the evening and overnight hours \nbefore becoming pseudo-stationary over central MO, some isolated\nconvection could develop, potentially becoming strong to \nmarginally severe. There's still a lot of details that need to \nbe ironed out as this is still several days away, however the \nbetter chances (30-60%) look to be overnight Wednesday into \nThursday morning. I wouldn't be surprised if a Marginal risk for\nsevere weather was introduced once we get into the Day 3 \noutlook period, as SPC continues to discuss this potential. \nHowever confidence and coverage remain too low for any certainty\nat this time. \n\nAdditional spokes of upper level energy will continue to push\nthrough the region through Friday, bringing more chances\n(30-50%) of showers and storms. The aforementioned stationary\nfront looks to retreat back to the north on Friday, so there\nwon't be a true frontal passage through our area with this\nsystem (as the previous discussion mentioned). \n\nLooking beyond the forecast period, the Ozarks continues to be\nhighlighted in a Moderate Risk (40-60%) of Extreme Heat by CPC\nduring the 8-14 day period, leading to above- normal \ntemperatures likely returning as we head into mid-August.",
